-- title:
--   Non-vanishing of dual (3,1) local zeta integrals at large real points
-- statement:
--   Fix a finite place $p$ of $\mathbb{Q}$, i.e. a height-one prime of $\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{Q}}$, and write $\mathbb{Q}_p$ for `p.adicCompletion ℚ` and $\mathrm{GL}_3(\mathbb{Q}_p)$ for `LocalGL3 p`. Let $W : \mathrm{GL}_3(\mathbb{Q}_p) \to \mathbb{C}$ satisfy: (i) the Whittaker transformation law for the inverse $\psi_p^{-1}$ of the standard local additive character `psiLocal`, namely $W(u(x,y,z)g) = \psi_p^{-1}(x+y)\,W(g)$ for all $x,y,z \in \mathbb{Q}_p$ and all $g$, where $u(x,y,z)$ is the upper unipotent matrix with superdiagonal entries $x,y$ and corner entry $z$; (ii) right invariance under some open subgroup $U_v \le \mathrm{GL}_3(\mathbb{Q}_p)$; (iii) $W \neq 0$; (iv) the irreducibility condition that every non-zero element $W'$ of the $\mathbb{C}$-span `gl3CyclicSubspace W` of the right translates of $W$ regenerates $W$, i.e. $W$ lies in the corresponding span for $W'$. Let $\eta : \mathbb{Q}_p^{\times} \to \mathbb{C}^{\times}$ be a character and $c_\eta \in \mathbb{N}$ a conductor exponent for it in the sense of `HasConductorExponentAt`: $\eta$ is trivial on the $c_\eta$-th higher unit group, and for each $m < c_\eta$ some unit in the $m$-th higher unit group has $\eta$-value $\neq 1$. Assume finally that for every $g \in \mathrm{GL}_3(\mathbb{Q}_p)$ there is $\sigma_1 \in \mathbb{R}$ such that for all $s$ with $\operatorname{Re} s > \sigma_1$ the function $(a,x) \mapsto \widetilde{W}\big(\iota(\mathrm{diag}(a,1))\,n_{21}(x)\,w'\,{}^{t}g^{-1}\big)\,\eta^{-1}(a)\,|a|^{s-1}$ is integrable for the product of the multiplicative measure `Measure.comap Units.val (mulMeasure (selfDualHaarAt ℚ p))` on $\mathbb{Q}_p^{\times}$ and the self-dual additive Haar measure `selfDualHaarAt ℚ p` on $\mathbb{Q}_p$; here $\widetilde{W}(h) = W(w_3\,{}^{t}h^{-1})$ is `dualWhittakerFn3 W`, $n_{21}(x)$ is the lower unipotent matrix with $(2,1)$ entry $x$, and $w'$ is the Weyl element `weylPrime3` permuting the last two coordinates. The conclusion is that there exists $g \in \mathrm{GL}_3(\mathbb{Q}_p)$ such that for every $\sigma \in \mathbb{R}$ there is a real $t > \sigma$ with $\mathrm{localZetaDual31}$ at $s = t$ non-zero, i.e. the dual $(3,1)$ zeta integral `localZeta31` formed from $\widetilde{W}$, $\eta^{-1}$ and the group element $w'\,{}^{t}g^{-1}$, with these two measures, does not vanish at $t$. Thus the non-vanishing obtained is only along arbitrarily large real points of some half-plane, not at a prescribed $s$.
--
--   This is the non-vanishing statement for local Rankin–Selberg zeta integrals of type $(3,1)$ on the contragredient side, for the irreducible generic representation of $\mathrm{GL}_3(\mathbb{Q}_p)$ generated by a Whittaker function, in the form used by Jacquet–Piatetski-Shapiro–Shalika. It feeds the local functional-equation step, being cited by [`LanglandsTunnell.CubicInduction.ne_zero_and_ne_zero_of_forall_localZeta30_eq_inv_eval_mul_and_localZetaDual31_eq_inv_eval_mul`](thm.html#LanglandsTunnell.CubicInduction.ne_zero_and_ne_zero_of_forall_localZeta30_eq_inv_eval_mul_and_localZetaDual31_eq_inv_eval_mul), where both a $(3,0)$ integral and its dual $(3,1)$ partner must be known to be non-zero somewhere far to the right.
